From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

Dr. Friedman, I think your basic premise in this argument is wrong.

AI has not "failed".  It has merely not succeeded as well, yet, as certain
enthusiasts for it have claimed.

Nevertheless, its successes have been quite substantial.  The accomplishments
of AI programs have included:

* Playing grandmaster-level chess.

* Proving many theorems of geometry as efficiently as trained humans can.

* Diagnosing lung diseases from lab results with accuracy exceeding the
  average for human doctors.

* Designing integrated circuits at mask level from high-level specifications.

* Generating and proving original and nontrivial theorems in the mathematics
  of Diophantine equations.

Computers now routinely perform these and many other tasks which used to be
thought of as the exclusive domain of human intelligence and creativity.

The reason for the widespread canard that AI has failed is that, when AI-derived
technology captures a task, that task gets almost instantly redefined as "not
AI".  Thus, AI never includes anything but research efforts and failures. :-)

The first major technology to get redefined as "not AI" was *compilers*.  Yes.
Back in the 1950s and early '60s, high-level languages were an AI research area
(it was called "automatic programming").

Several major technologies (including, for example, pick-and-place robots) have
gone through the same evolution since.

Most recently, "expert systems" got redefined as not-AI. Next, I suspect, will
be neural networks.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

