Well, this sounds even more specious the way Adler puts it. In fact,
I claim that this contention quite clearly begs the question. What he
has given is a description of a "hard" engineering problem in some
sense.

"The possibility of their success" is Boolean. It is possible to build
an intelligent machine or it is not (assuming also that we've agreed
on criteria to recognize an AI when we see one [gee, wasn't there just
a thread on that?], which is non-trivial[1]). In unlimited time, the
"probability" of "humans-achieve-a-given-level-of-AI" is 1 or 0,
regardless of the current state of their attempts. This is equivalent
to the somewhat idealistic statement that there either is, or is not,
a Turing-test-competent Turing machine in the space of all Turing
machines (unless Church's Hypothesis is invalid).

\begin{speculate}
Since the Turing test that Adler mentions is linguistic, and could be
done by our hypothetical serial ASCII-bot, I give the following
existance proof (wherein a strong AI proponent gleefully embraces
the Chinese Room):

Pick an intelligent human (Alice) of finite lifespan. Record all
sentences spoken to or read by Alice, which is a finite set I.
Similarly, record Alice's spoken or written responses, another finite
set O. Write a retrieval program P, which responds, "Could you rephrase
that?" or some other such phrase on every retrieval failure. This
program and its database are finite, if rather obnoxiously large. Now
repeat for the (finite) set of all humans who have ever lived; you
still have a finite program P_u, and it's pretty clear that this
machine will pass a Turing test -- although finding this machine is
another variation on looking something up in the Universal Library.

Exercise 1: Assume that you've found P_u. Optimize it ;-)
\end{speculate}
