Consider the writing of Mortimer Adler concerning this thread:
>From _Intellect: Mind Over Matter_, Macmillan, 1990, pp.66-67.

	[...]

	Holding the view that I have so far expressed -- that the brain
	is not the organ of thought and that an immaterial factor in
	the human mind is required in order for its universal concepts
	to confer significance on the general terms we use in ordinary
	discourse -- I regard it as highly improbable that a Turing
	machine will ever be built that is able to succeed in passing
	the Turing test.

	Highly improbable does not mean impossible. To get at the tuth
	about this matter, it is of the utmost importance for the
	computer techologist to keep on trying to produce the machine
	that Turing claimed is, in principle, mathematically possible.

	Philosophical arguments frequently fail to persuade the
	opponents at which they are directed.  I have good reason to
	doubt that the arguments I have directed against the moderate
	materialists at the end of the preceding chapter and at the
	beginning of this one will persuade them that the brain may be
	the necessary, but cannot be the sufficient, condition of
	conceptual thought.

	How else might they be persuaded of the soundness of my view,
	if in fact is is correct?  By the failure of the computer
	technologists to build the requisite AI machine--one that can
	perform as Turing claimed it should be able to.  Each time the
	technologists try and fail, the possibility of their success
	becomes less and less probable.  Each failure increases the
	probability that machines will never be able to perform in a
	manner indistinquishable from that of the human mind.

	The answer may not be forthcoming in the immediate future, but
	the pursuit of truth is an unending process involving the whole
	of available time.  Up to the present moment, machines have not
	turned in the requisite performance.  Reasons can be given for
	thinking that they nerver will.  I an content to let matters
	stand that way.  I hope my readers feel the same.

This passage appears in the chapter "Artificial Intelligence and the
Human Intellect".  When I originally read this I had several misgivings
(I actually read this prior to David Freeman's initial posting but was
not prepared to refute the claim openly).  So David should at least
feel he is not alone in this belief.  Carefully re-read Adler's
contention:

mja>	Each time the technologists try and fail, the possibility of
mja>	their success becomes less and less probable.  Each failure
mja>	increases the probability that machines will never be able to
mja>	perform in a manner indistinquishable from that of the human
mja>	mind.

This appears to be the same argument used in David's original posting.
I am not yet ready to respond in detail on this thread but I thought it
would be "unethical" to withhold such a reference.  I'm not appealing
to authority, I'm just giving some additional credibility to David's
original remarks, reprinted here for convenience:

	[...]

ddf>	Consider the history of AI as a series of such experiments. It
ddf>	is my understanding (I claim no expertise in the subject) that
ddf>	proponent of AI have consistently overestimated what they are
ddf>	going to be able to accomplish.  If achieving what they said
ddf>	they would would have been evidence for their views, then
ddf>	failing to achieve it is evidence against.  Put a little
ddf>	differently, predictions of what AI would accomplish were
ddf>	deduced from a set of assumptions, one of which was that
ddf>	consciousness could be adequately described as mechanism. The
ddf>	failure of such predictions is then evidence against all of the
ddf>	assumptions--including that one. Of course, if you start out
ddf>	very sure of that assumption, you will attribute almost all of
ddf>	the failure to failure of other assumptions--but unless you are
ddf>	certain of the assumption, some of the weight of the failure
ddf>	has to count against it.

ddf>	Does anyone see a hole in the argument?

	
I must confess that after completing my "one" class in AI (16 years
ago) I had ~more~ appreciation of the human mind than prior to taking
the class.  We primarily dealt with "vision systems" and "scene
parsing".  Just taken alone, human vision will make almost all theories
(including Darwin) seem incomplete or too simplistic.  Incidently, 16
years ago, perceptrons were out and fuzzy logic was not being allowed
in.  Now it appears that with some refinements that perceptrons (in the
form of neural nets) and fuzzy logic are being allowed back into AI.
Please no flames from the AI researchers, I know that ~neural nets~ and
~perceptrons~ are not the same thing.

More later.

