From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

Hmm, I guess we'll have to go back to basics.  

Hal Finney argues that "homesteading is arguably the natural way
ownership is created", while "An auction requires an auctioneer, and a
seller" and " Auctions seem to require a police force which is
patrolling the universe to enforce the deeds to land."  Tim Starr
similarly argues that "this requires that the universe already be
owned by someone" and Lefty asks "from whom would one _buy_ a portion
of the atmosphere?"

Property is a Schelling point.  People respect property rights if they
think it is their interest to do so, given what they expect others to
do.  A stable system of property rights has owners invoking sufficient
threats of retaliation to discourage most rights violators.  *Any*
allocation of property rights will likely have some analogy of
"police", third parties hired to help enforce rights.  And larger
groups of people are likely to cooperate in hiring "police"; if they
allow the violation of someone else's rights to go unchallenged, their
rights may not be safe.  

The question is, what processes can create such interlocking
expectations about who will defend what rights how much?  And given a
choice of several such processes, which should we prefer?
"Naturalness", whatever that means, should only be given so much
weight.  No one needs to own something now for an auction to be able
to create rights.  What is needed is shared expectations that people
will defend these newly created rights.

As Nick Szabo mentioned, "under the "Freedom of the Skies" treaty, no
nation can claim sovereignty of any resource beyond the Earth's
atmosphere.  ...  outer space is an anarchy, ... I doubt it will stay
that way for long."  There may soon be a window of opportunity for a
politically feasible agreement whereby "space is the common heritage
of mankind" is understood to mean that (the governments of) all
mankind would be given the proceeds of an auction selling off the moon
or larger regions.

Poor countries who stood to gain auction proceeds would want to commit
to supporting those rights.  And rich countries with influential
citizens hoping to buy and develop parts of the universe would have
incentives to affirm and defend those rights, so that their rights
would be protected.  And people buying in an auction would have
incentives to not buy more than they could plausibly defend, even with
the aid of others, from those who violated such a treaty.

The objection I take more seriously is Hal's point that "we won't know
the [proper units of ownership] answer until people live there, and by
then it's too late for the auction."  I do however agree with Nick
that Hal may be too optimistic in suggesting that "once people are out
there, if they decide that they need "sunlight rights" or "periastron
slots" or whatever else it turns out is natural, they can create these
with court systems much as we create rights to quiet, unobstructed
views, or clean air today."  More likely we'd just be stuck with be
less than optimal rights. 

Robin Hanson

