Craig Presson says:
> In <9305251621.AA00375@wookumz.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, "Perry E. Metzger" writes:
> |> 
> [Perry summarizes all of neuroscience]
> |> Now, where could a "soul" hide in all this? I can't see a space for
> |> it. [...]
> 
> The "space" for it resides in the complexity[1]. That is, we see two
> things that we commonsensically can't explain: our sense of a single,
> continuing "self"[2] amid all the chaos of daily activity, and the
> feeling that we are able to do or aspire to such great things that
> there must be "divinity" in us (the Cartesian argument). Or you could
> generalize that to our ability to symbolize and our sense of wonder,
> respectively. Thus the "space" exists only in our understanding of the
> nervous system, not in the system itself. As you so well point out, we
> keep taking the "machine" apart to finer and finer degrees without
> finding the "ghost", to the point where those who wish to reserve a
> space for it are reduced to talking about unattested quantum effects.

Which is precisely my point. Every time we try to examine any of the
function of the brain we come up with something mechanistic at every
level, from the cognitive people who find really strange effects that
are only sensible if there is an underlying cognitive system based on
mechanistic principles, to the people mapping neurons. In fact, I can
pick any part of the brain I like, examine the neurons in it, and I'll
find they are behaving according to a reasonable mechanistic model.

Indeed, the Penrosian "quantum waving" is largely bull, too, because
quantum effects are as modelable as anything else -- given a computer
and a random number generator I can simulate a quantum mechanical
driven system as well as I like.

